  "question": {
    "text": "The difference in the number of waves when&nbsp;yellow light propagates through air and vacuum columns of the same thickness is one. The thickness of the air column is _____&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>mm</mi></math>. [Refractive index of air <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>0003</mn></math>, the wavelength of yellow light in vacuum <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>6000</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mover><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi><mo>&#8728;</mo></mover><mo>]</mo></math>",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [],
  "correctValue": "2",
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>Thickness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>&#955;</mi></math></p><p>So, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>n</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mrow><mi>vac</mi><mo>&#160;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mo>(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>)</mo><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mrow><mi>air</mi><mo>&#160;</mo></mrow></msub></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>n</mi><mi>&#955;</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>)</mo><mfrac><mi>&#955;</mi><msub><mi>&#956;</mi><mi>air</mi></msub></mfrac></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><msub><mi>&#956;</mi><mi>air</mi></msub><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>4</mn></msup><mn>3</mn></mfrac></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>&#955;</mi></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>4</mn></msup><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>6000</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mover><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi><mn>0</mn></mover></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>mm</mi></math></p>",
